    "body": "The obvious answer would be to use `@` with no spacing or `in` between the username and the hostname. But that becomes a whole can of worms because it requires altering the user format as well and there's no conditional formatting as far as I'm aware (other than to show hostname format always or only in ssh). I've relegated to manually doing that for my own config on individual hosts that I regularly remote into as I'll never use those hosts locally. \n\nThe only other I can think of is simply `ssh `. \n\nI kept it empty because it seemed like the `in ` from the user format seemed descriptive enough. For example:\n\n```\nuser in host in ~/desktop\n```",
    "created_at": "2024-07-26T02:27:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-07-26T02:29:26Z",
